WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Constructing a Metrics Conversion Table

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.433
Welcome this session on performance benchmarking.

3
00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:14.266
What we have in front of us is a simple little data table for a number of CTQ’s.

4
00:00:14.266 --> 00:00:20.832
In this case, scroll over to it, we have four CTQ’s.

5
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:27.799
CTQ 1 is measured in yield; whereas CTQ 2, defects per unit;

6
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:33.200
three is Z short-term, that's Sigma capability;

7
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.400
and Cp, a process capability ratio.

8
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:42.466
The nature of those metrics is yield as a long-term, as is DPU, they’re both long-term metrics.

9
00:00:42.466 --> 00:00:46.466
That's because that they are discrete databased.

10
00:00:46.466 --> 00:00:49.999
They take a long time to collect that kind of data.

11
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.400
To get a reasonably accurate estimate of their inherent capability.

12
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:59.733
Where as short-term Z.st and Cp are continuous measures, as we'll see,

13
00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:02.733
and therefore there short-term.

14
00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:08.299
Again, as we look at this example of performance benchmarking,

15
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:12.300
don't feel that you have a need to understand all the calculations right now.

16
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:19.066
What's important is the concept. What we’re doing is CTQ 1 is apples, DPU is oranges,

17
00:01:19.066 --> 00:01:24.032
pears, and peaches. So, what we want to do is get them all the pineapple. Heh.

18
00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:30.999
So, what we’re going to do is do that. We find that the yield of CTQ1 is 99%.

19
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.533
The DPU is 1, Z is 3 Sigma, and Cp is 1.45.

20
00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:39.599
This Op is the number of opportunities.

21
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:45.700
So there are 4 opportunities that came together to get the yield of 99%.

22
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:51.200
In other words, CTQ 1 appeared four times in the design.

23
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:58.133
CTQ 2 appeared three times, CTQ 3 twice, and CTQ 4 appears six times.

24
00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:02.033
Now, what we have to do to get Z.bench, is where we’re headed,

25
00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:04.999
is to get a common base of computation.

26
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.000
In this case, we’re going to convert all of our metrics

27
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.933
from long-term and short-term into Y.norm,

28
00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:19.366
and then we’re going to convert them to Sigma's.

29
00:02:19.366 --> 00:02:22.999
The yield or Y.norm into Sigma.

30
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.066
Now, we do that with a simple equation here.

31
00:02:26.066 --> 00:02:29.399
I'm not going to dwell on it because we'll be talking about it later.

32
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:35.800
Basically, what I do is take yield. In this case yield is already in yield, D2.

33
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:40.933
Raise it to 1 over 4, the opportunity count.

34
00:02:40.933 --> 00:02:46.099
99 7 4. 99.74 percent yield

35
00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:51.366
of four things will roll through to a final yield of 99%.

36
00:02:51.366 --> 00:02:57.366
Of course, a DPU of one, three opportunities each,

37
00:02:57.366 --> 00:03:01.366
then the opportunity yield is 66.67%.

38
00:03:01.366 --> 00:03:05.766
Z short-term of 3, 2 such opportunities,

39
00:03:05.766 --> 00:03:10.432
the yield is 99.93% per opportunity.

40
00:03:10.433 --> 00:03:16.533
And, if I have six things, each with a Cp of 1.45,

41
00:03:16.533 --> 00:03:23.566
then the yield per opportunity is 99.998%.

42
00:03:23.566 --> 00:03:27.566
Then I convert those to Sigma.

43
00:03:27.566 --> 00:03:34.432
And I get this one by adding 1.5, because remember, this was long-term.

44
00:03:34.433 --> 00:03:39.333
Again, I add 1.5 because it's long-term.

45
00:03:39.333 --> 00:03:43.166
Here I add nothing. See, there's nothing on the end.

46
00:03:43.166 --> 00:03:46.266
Because the originating data was short-term by nature.

47
00:03:46.266 --> 00:03:51.099
Same thing here. I add nothing on the end.

48
00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:57.966
So what I've done, is I have converted everything to a common metric, yield.

49
00:03:57.966 --> 00:04:06.166
And then normalized it, per opportunity. And then converted the normalized yield to a Sigma.

50
00:04:06.166 --> 00:04:09.532
Correcting for whether it's short-term or long-term,

51
00:04:09.533 --> 00:04:15.966
putting everything on a short-term basis. So, all four of these are short-term.

52
00:04:15.966 --> 00:04:20.999
Then I convert the Sigma back into yield.

53
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:25.800
Find the product of the four yields.

54
00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:33.533
Raise to one over 4, because there are four of those right there.

55
00:04:33.533 --> 00:04:37.733
Then convert back into Sigma.

56
00:04:37.733 --> 00:04:42.899
So, I'm going from Sigma to yield, to normalized yield, back to Sigma.

57
00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:46.900
That is Z.benchmark. I’ll recolor that.

58
00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:52.800
That is Z.benchmark, right there.

59
00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.266
Put that in black, 2.46 Sigma.

60
00:04:56.266 --> 00:05:02.966
So, what that’s saying is, very simple. Zooming out.

61
00:05:02.966 --> 00:05:09.732
Given this data, as highlighted.

62
00:05:09.733 --> 00:05:16.733
Four things ranging from 1.93 Sigma to 4.7 Sigma.

63
00:05:16.733 --> 00:05:23.699
The typical Sigma per opportunity or what we call Z.bench is 2.46 Sigma.

64
00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:30.266
Now, we can easily confirm that, and we will later.

65
00:05:30.266 --> 00:05:36.766
But right now recognize that's how we go out and do benchmarking among dissimilar things.

66
00:05:36.766 --> 00:05:41.766
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
